1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,784
What should a service do when a setting is missing?

2
00:00:02,834 --> 00:00:07,536
In Lesson 5, ecom and the card zone talked only through a written contract.

3
00:00:07,586 --> 00:00:13,440
In Lesson 4, each product had its own database, its own keys and its own logins.

4
00:00:13,490 --> 00:00:19,584
This lesson shows where every key and setting comes from, and what happens when one is missing.

5
00:00:19,634 --> 00:00:26,112
Picture a payment service with a timeout written in its settings file: thirty seconds, as a sensible default.

6
00:00:26,162 --> 00:00:31,992
In production, nobody sets the real value, so thirty seconds quietly stays.

7
00:00:32,042 --> 00:00:35,328
One day the card network takes forty seconds to answer.

8
00:00:35,378 --> 00:00:38,880
The service gives up at thirty and tells the shop the payment failed.

9
00:00:38,930 --> 00:00:42,264
But the card network said yes, and the buyer's money is gone.

10
00:00:42,314 --> 00:00:45,168
A guessed default looked safe until production.

11
00:00:45,218 --> 00:00:47,040
A good cook works the other way.

12
00:00:47,090 --> 00:00:50,712
If an ingredient is missing, the cook stops the dish.

13
00:00:50,762 --> 00:00:52,584
The cook never guesses a pinch.

14
00:00:52,634 --> 00:00:54,456
Our services are the same.

15
00:00:54,506 --> 00:00:58,008
The rule is simple: no setting has a default value.

16
00:00:58,058 --> 00:01:03,528
Every setting comes from one locked store, Vault: a safe for settings and secrets.

17
00:01:03,578 --> 00:01:05,592
Three kinds of value go into it.

18
00:01:05,642 --> 00:01:09,960
Some belong to the system itself, like which service listens where.

19
00:01:10,010 --> 00:01:16,200
Some are secrets, like passwords and keys, and every one of them is generated inside Vault.

20
00:01:16,250 --> 00:01:20,448
Nobody types a password into a file, and the product ships none.

21
00:01:20,498 --> 00:01:25,224
And some only this bank knows, like the card network's address and the bank's login there.

22
00:01:25,274 --> 00:01:28,728
The operator types each of those once, into Vault.

23
00:01:28,778 --> 00:01:35,544
Before a service starts, it is handed exactly its own settings, in a private file only it can read.

24
00:01:35,594 --> 00:01:39,984
Its own configuration holds only the names of settings, never the values.

25
00:01:40,034 --> 00:01:44,088
If even one value is missing, the service refuses to start.

26
00:01:44,138 --> 00:01:47,736
It names every missing one at once, not just the first.

27
00:01:47,786 --> 00:01:52,968
That is fail fast: stop loudly at the start, instead of failing quietly later.

28
00:01:53,018 --> 00:01:56,184
The same rule covers the bank's business decisions.

29
00:01:56,234 --> 00:02:04,248
Rates, limits and waiting times are the bank's choices, so each is a setting the bank chooses, never a number hidden in the product.

30
00:02:04,298 --> 00:02:08,376
The card zone has its own settings too, and every one is the bank's.

31
00:02:08,426 --> 00:02:13,824
The bank chooses which systems may call the card zone, and which roles each caller gets.

32
00:02:13,874 --> 00:02:22,152
For each caller, the bank chooses which websites may show the card form, how long a card-entry session lives, and how many tries a buyer gets.

33
00:02:22,202 --> 00:02:31,464
The bank chooses the fraud limits: how many attempts one card, one group of cards, one address and one shop may make within its chosen time window.

34
00:02:31,514 --> 00:02:38,544
The bank chooses how long an unused card is kept before it is deleted, and how often the keys that lock card numbers change.

35
00:02:38,594 --> 00:02:41,856
And the bank chooses when the nightly clean-up jobs run.

36
00:02:41,906 --> 00:02:43,728
Now watch the product.

37
00:02:43,778 --> 00:02:47,304
Settings have no screen, so watch how the system behaves.

38
00:02:47,354 --> 00:02:49,656
The operator installs the card zone.

39
00:02:49,706 --> 00:02:56,568
Vault holds the system's values and the generated secrets, but one caller's fraud limit has not been typed in yet.

40
00:02:56,618 --> 00:02:59,088
The install stops before anything runs.

41
00:02:59,138 --> 00:03:03,216
Its message names the missing value, and the caller it belongs to.

42
00:03:03,266 --> 00:03:06,216
The operator types it in and runs the install again.

43
00:03:06,266 --> 00:03:10,272
Now take one service on its own, started with nothing set at all.

44
00:03:10,322 --> 00:03:12,144
It does not start.

45
00:03:12,194 --> 00:03:15,600
It prints one message: required settings are missing.

46
00:03:15,650 --> 00:03:19,272
Below it comes one line for every missing setting, by name.

47
00:03:19,322 --> 00:03:21,144
Nothing half-works.

48
00:03:21,194 --> 00:03:25,800
Nothing listens with a guessed address, a test password or a made-up timeout.

49
00:03:25,850 --> 00:03:28,992
Then its settings are handed over, and the service starts.

50
00:03:29,042 --> 00:03:30,864
What does this protect?

51
00:03:30,914 --> 00:03:36,792
A wrong guess can never reach production, so a payment never fails on a timeout nobody chose.

52
00:03:36,842 --> 00:03:42,048
A test password can never guard real money, because no password exists outside Vault.

53
00:03:42,098 --> 00:03:49,128
And the bank stays in charge: every limit, window and time in the product is a value the bank chose and can see.

54
00:03:49,178 --> 00:03:53,136
That protects the buyer's money, and the bank's control over its own system.

55
00:03:53,186 --> 00:03:55,008
Back to the cook.

56
00:03:55,058 --> 00:03:58,056
Every ingredient on the counter, or no dish at all.

57
00:03:58,106 --> 00:04:03,768
Like the cook, the service won't guess: every value comes from Vault, or it doesn't start.

58
00:04:03,818 --> 00:04:06,960
Next: how every call proves who it comes from.
